\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Perplexity over the scoring suffix versus accuracy for every method and run in the audit protocol (log scale; the superseded v1.1 runs are excluded). All points use the suffix-scoring protocol, so the identity control is the student's suffix-native reference; the student's full-context native loss (25.4, Table~\ref {tab:main}) is a different quantity and is not plotted. The identity control sits at low perplexity and high accuracy, while translated caches, probe mixtures, and native content spread across the plane---including a point with near-native perplexity ($23.7$) whose accuracy stays $23$ points below the student's $0.500$. Perplexity does not order methods the way the task metric does. The channel ablation (key-only versus value-only versus both) is reported in Appendix~\ref {app:channel}.}}{9}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:decoupling}{{4}{9}{Perplexity over the scoring suffix versus accuracy for every method and run in the audit protocol (log scale; the superseded v1.1 runs are excluded). All points use the suffix-scoring protocol, so the identity control is the student's suffix-native reference; the student's full-context native loss (25.4, Table~\ref {tab:main}) is a different quantity and is not plotted. The identity control sits at low perplexity and high accuracy, while translated caches, probe mixtures, and native content spread across the plane---including a point with near-native perplexity ($23.7$) whose accuracy stays $23$ points below the student's $0.500$. Perplexity does not order methods the way the task metric does. The channel ablation (key-only versus value-only versus both) is reported in Appendix~\ref {app:channel}}{figure.4}{}}
